          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the last word of the chapter: what did chāgaḥ mean, and what did vānaraḥ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: हंसः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "हंसः (haṁsaḥ) — \"a goose\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "हंसः (haṁsaḥ) is a goose, and in poetry a swan."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Another secure cousin, as far-travelled as वृकः (vṛkaḥ): Latin ānser, Greek khēn, German Gans, English goose and gander. The bird held on to its name as faithfully as the wolf did."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In Sanskrit poetry it is the bird that can take milk out of water, so the word came to carry a sense of a mind that sorts things — and दुग्धम् is already yours to put beside it. Ends in -अः."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five animals: a lion, a wolf, a goat, a monkey, a goose."
            }
          ]
        },
